-### [Voxel Coloring](4418-voxel-coloring.md)
-A volumetric reconstruction algorithm using color consistency to distinguish object surface voxels from background. It distinguishes itself by starting with an opaque volume and carving inconsistent voxels, rather than integrating density along rays.
+A CPU-optimized implementation of the Feldkamp-Davis-Kress (FDK) algorithm, an analytical method for reconstructing a 3D volume from a stack of 2D cone-beam projection images. It solves the tomographic reconstruction problem by applying a specific convolution filter to weighted projections and back-projecting them into a 3D grid. It sets itself apart from its siblings by precomputing geometric weights and using a direct spatial-domain convolution filter to avoid the overhead of FFTs during the main reconstruction loop, prioritizing speed and memory locality on CPU architectures.
 
 ### [HoloINR](4745-holoinr.md)
-Solves the coupled inverse problem of holotomography (holography and tomography) using Implicit Neural Representations (INRs) for near-field setups. It distinguishes itself by modeling Fresnel propagation physics between object and detector, unlike far-field parallel/fan-beam assumptions.
+Solves the coupled inverse problem of near-field holotomography by combining holographic phase retrieval with tomographic reconstruction using Implicit Neural Representations (INRs). Unlike standard tomography that assumes far-field (Fraunhofer) propagation, HoloINR explicitly models near-field physics via Fresnel propagation between the object and the detector. It differs from sibling methods like NeAT or DuDoTrans by replacing discrete voxel grids with a continuous neural field, allowing for resolution-independent reconstruction and natural integration of wave-optics effects.
 
 ### [EmissivityRenderer](4811-emissivityrenderer.md)
-A differentiable renderer for multi-line neural tomography recovering 3-D electron density and temperature from spectral-intensity images. It distinguishes itself by performing Line-of-Sight (LOS) integration of emissivity, specific to plasma physics applications.
