+A differentiable renderer designed for multi-line neural tomography, specifically recovering 3-D coronal electron density ($n_e$) and temperature ($T_e$) from multiview, multiline spectral-intensity images. It performs ray marching and Line-of-Sight (LOS) integration of emissivity coefficients derived from neural network models. It is distinguished from general volume renderers by its explicit physics-based emissivity computation using a Look-Up Table (LUT) for ion-specific spectral lines and its integration with occupancy grids for sparse volume representation.
 
 ### [R2-Gaussian](6090-r2-gaussian.md)
-Adapts 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) for tomography by addressing integration bias. It uses a local affine approximation to map 3D Gaussians to 2D Gaussians on the detector for alpha compositing. It distinguishes itself by enabling efficient, differentiable rendering of Gaussian primitives for CT reconstruction.
+A tomographic reconstruction method that adapts 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) to sparse-view X-ray imaging by rectifying an integration bias inherent in standard 3DGS splatting. It replaces the appearance-based 3DGS kernel with a radiative Gaussian kernel parameterized by density, position, and covariance, and derives new X-ray rendering functions to ensure accurate volume retrieval. Unlike siblings that use 3DGS only for data augmentation or replace rasterization with simulators, R2-Gaussian maintains efficient differentiable rasterization while enabling direct 3D density volume extraction.
 
 ### [Differential Rendering for CT Reconstruction](6462-differential-rendering-for-ct-reconstruction.md)
-An iterative method optimizing 3D volume parameters (absorption coefficient $\sigma$) by minimizing MSE between differentially rendered images and reference projections. It distinguishes itself by explicitly using the gradient of the rendering equation with respect to volume parameters for optimization.
+An inverse rendering technique that reconstructs the volumetric absorption coefficients of an object from a series of 2D X-ray projection images by optimizing scene parameters via gradient descent. It formulates Computed Tomography (CT) as an iterative optimization problem where the 3D volume is treated as a differentiable parameter space, allowing the use of auto-differentiable rendering engines to compute gradients of a projection loss. Unlike traditional algebraic or analytical methods, this approach relies on end-to-end differentiability of the forward projection, enabling the use of modern optimizers like ADAM without explicit derivation of the back-projection operator. It is distinct from neural implicit methods (e.g., [HoloINR](4745-holoinr.md)) by typically operating on explicit voxel grids rather than continuous neural fields, and from deep learning approaches by requiring no pre-trained models.
 
-### Density Reconstruction (Absorption-Only)
-Reconstruction of a 3D density field from images using an optical absorption-only model, reducing the problem to a linear optimization problem solvable via DiffDVR [S2]. It uses multi-resolution optimization, starting at $32^3$ and doubling resolution every 10 iterations, performing 50 iterations at the highest resolution ($256^3$) [S2]. The loss function uses a smoothing prior on volume densities with $\lambda = 0.5$ [S2]. Parameters include 64 uniformly sampled views on a surrounding sphere, $512^2$ image resolution, Adam optimizer with batch size 8 and learning rate 0.3, and a step size of 0.2 voxels [S2]. Performance comparison for a human skull dataset shows ASTRA takes 53 seconds, DiffDVR takes ~12 minutes, and Mitsuba takes multiple hours [S2].
+### [Algebraic Reconstruction Technique (ART)](6944-algebraic-reconstruction-technique-art.md)
+An iterative algebraic method that minimizes the quadratic norm of the difference between acquired projections and projected reconstructed volume, avoiding explicit matrix storage.
 
-### Space-Time Image Formation Model
-A model representing a continuously deforming 3D object as a 4D density function $h(x, y, z, t)$, discretized into $N_k$ frames, each represented by a voxel grid of $N_v$ voxels, assuming each frame is approximately static for reconstruction [S3, unofficial]. The tomography problem is expressed as $A \cdot f = p$, where $A$ is a block diagonal matrix with blocks $A_t \in \mathbb{R}^{(N_\theta M \times N_v)}$ modeling the Radon transform for $N_\theta$ projections contributing to frame $t$, $f$ is the vector of 3D volumes, and $p$ is the vector of measured sinogram data $p_t \in \mathbb{R}^{(N_\theta M)}$ [S3, unofficial]. Parameters: $N_k \le N_p$ (number of reconstructed frames), $N_\theta = N_p/N_k$ (projections per frame), $M$ (pixels per projection) [S3, unofficial]. The 4D density volume is described by a vector $f \in \mathbb{R}^{(N_v N_k)}$, composed of column vectors $f_t \in \mathbb{R}^{N_v}$ for each time step $t$, where $f = \{f_0, f_1, ..., f_{N_k-1}\}$ [S3, unofficial].
+### [Dynamic Tomographic Reconstruction with Motion Identification (DynTomo)](7376-dynamic-tomographic-reconstruction-with-motion-identificatio.md)
+A staggered two-step iterative process that alternates between reconstructing a volume from a frozen displacement field and identifying the motion from a frozen estimate of the microstructure to handle deforming volumes in tomography.
+
+### [Inverse Tomographic Display](7467-inverse-tomographic-display.md)
+A volumetric display where multiple emitters surround the display area and project rays with programmed intensity for each direction; the intersection of rays in a scattering medium produces the graphics.
+
+### [Iterative Wavelet Thresholding for CT Reconstruction](8259-iterative-wavelet-thresholding-for-ct-reconstruction.md)
+An iterative reconstruction method that solves the inverse problem $g = Af + n$ by combining a gradient descent step on the data fidelity term with a sparsity-promoting wavelet thresholding step. It is used to reconstruct images from noisy sinograms, particularly in sparse or limited angle scenarios.
+
+### [Deep Learning and Neural Architectures](8282-deep-learning-and-neural-architectures.md)
+This family encompasses methods that replace or augment traditional algebraic regularizers with neural networks to solve the inverse problem of tomographic reconstruction. It distinguishes itself from classical iterative methods (like SIRT or ART) by leveraging the expressive power of deep learning to enforce priors, either implicitly through network architecture (Deep Image Prior) or explicitly through learned features (Transformers), often enabling reconstruction from sparse or noisy data where traditional methods fail.
+
+### [Physics-Based Image Formation Models](8344-physics-based-image-formation-models.md)
+Physics-Based Image Formation Models define the differentiable forward operator $\mathcal{P}$ that maps a 3D (or 4D) volume representation to 2D projection images, serving as the core component in inverse rendering pipelines. Unlike generic differentiable renderers, these models are tailored to specific physical acquisition modalities (e.g., X-ray absorption, ultrasound wave propagation) to ensure physical consistency and computational efficiency. They distinguish themselves from siblings like [Neural Adaptive Tomography](1862-neural-adaptive-tomography-neat.md) by explicitly modeling the underlying physics (such as linear absorption or coupled wave equations) rather than relying solely on learned neural priors or adaptive sampling strategies.
 
